fleeing on foot carrying all their  worldly possessions the highway out of  quarantine Bogota has become dotted with  hundreds of homeless hungry Venezuelans  attempting to walk back to their  battered country boots Alice and her  family worked as street vendors but with  the lockdown they ran out of money and  ended on the streets there was no one  left to sell to how were we supposed to  survive the police even prohibited us  from being outside  we live hand-to-mouth day by day how can  we pay the daily rent how can we eat in  recent years more than 4 million  Venezuelans fled to other South American  countries escaping the economic collapse  in Venezuela some had managed to earn a  meager but decent living like the Ries  who worked in a car wash leaving was an  agonizing decision there's nothing left  to do here we lost our jobs and we can't  count on getting help from the  government until two weeks ago when the  lockdown started Venezuelan migrants in  difficulty had access to some form of  assistance but since then shelters  migrant centers and soup kitchens have  all closed leaving them with no help at  all  the government has increased the number  of cash transfers to some struggling  families but it's not enough  Bogut as mayor said that at least for  now temporary shelters being built in  the city were exclusively for vulnerable  Colombians the doors of Catholic relief  organizations are closed with no  official date for reopening father will  frown alaya says the fear of contagion  and the excessive number of people in  need  left them with no other option no we  don't have the resources nor the space  to deal with this pressure we're saying  that the mayor and the government are  passing responsibility to one another  because the number of people our need  are simply too high back in the highway  there Louise stopped to catch a breath  and firm up their bags they estimate  there will take them 15 days to get to  the border they know the situation back  home will be equally tough but at least  they say they'll have a roof over their  heads and their family nearby to help as  much as they can  Allison Trump et al Jazeera Bogota
 

